# iOS Port Prototype

The current iOS port prototype is built in 2 parts. The core libraries such as
XUL are built as-normal, and then an xcode project is used to bundle these
libraries into an iOS application, which can be deployed to a device or
simulator.

Currently there is no support for running automated iOS tests, though this is
being investigated.

## Simulator

### Building

1. Update your .mozconfig as described below:

```
# Enable the iOS application target.
ac_add_options --enable-application=mobile/ios
```

2. Build the main core of XUL with `./mach build` as normal.

### Running

Currently the in-tree wrapper for Gecko on iOS is the minimal "GeckoTestBrowser"
application. To run this application, open the xcode project at
`mobile/ios/GeckoTestBrowser/GeckoTestBrowser.xcodeproj`, and build/run it from
there.

## Dejavu on iOS

In this tree, GeckoTestBrowser is the iOS version of Dejavu, the Android app in
`mobile/android/fenix` (see its `dejavu` packages). The Xcode project and its
targets keep their upstream names; the app shows as "Dejavu". Paths below are
relative to `mobile/ios/GeckoTestBrowser`.

### What it has

The Dejavu features of the Android app, rebuilt in SwiftUI on the Gecko views of
`GeckoView/`:

- Workspaces with names, emoji or Zen icons, color themes and a default
  container, swiped through on the home screen, and a private workspace.
- Pinned tabs, folders (nested up to 5 levels), essentials (optionally per
  container), drag and drop, selection of several tabs and their actions bar.
- Containers, temporary containers (created per tab, deleted with their data
  once their last tab closes) and moving tabs between containers.
- Custom actions (web requests run on tabs), the editable "More" menu and the
  actions bar below pages, split view, link menu entries (new tab, private tab,
  split view, container, workspace), links from other apps when Dejavu is the
  default browser, bookmarks and history.
- Syncing workspaces with Zen through a Mozilla account (Zen's "spaces" sync
  collection, the same records as the Android app).
- Dejavu's colors, icon, launch screen and all its translations.

Not there yet, because GeckoView for iOS cannot do them yet: add-ons, passwords,
downloads, translations, printing and saving pages as PDF. Texts that only the
iOS app has (`L10n+iOS.swift`, `L10n+Settings.swift`, `L10n+Sync.swift`) are in
English only.

### Where things are

- `GeckoTestBrowser/Dejavu/Model`: workspaces, containers, actions, settings,
  bookmarks and history, stored as JSON in Application Support/Dejavu in the
  same form as on Android.
- `GeckoTestBrowser/Dejavu/Browser`: tabs and their Gecko sessions, the
  browser screen, menus, search.
- `GeckoTestBrowser/Dejavu/Home`, `Settings`, `UI`: the home screen, settings
  and shared styles.
- `GeckoTestBrowser/Dejavu/Sync`: the Mozilla account and spaces sync, a port of
  the Android app's `dejavu/sync` package. The account's tokens and sync key
  are kept in the Keychain.
- `GeckoView/`: the Swift bindings, extended with containers, private mode,
  desktop mode, find in page, session restore and security information.

### Building

1. Build Gecko for iOS as described above (`ac_add_options
   --enable-application=mobile/ios`, plus `--target=aarch64-apple-ios` for a
   device, see `mobile/ios/config/mozconfigs`), with `./mach build`.
2. Open `GeckoTestBrowser.xcodeproj` in Xcode 16 or newer.
3. In "Signing & Capabilities", choose your own team for the GeckoTestBrowser,
   WebContent, Networking and Rendering targets. A browser with its own engine
   needs Apple's browser engine entitlements (see the `.entitlements` files);
   if you change the app's bundle identifier, change it in
   `WebContent/WebContent.entitlements` too.
4. Build and run the GeckoTestBrowser scheme.

### Regenerating translations and images

`tools/dejavu_l10n.py` makes `GeckoTestBrowser/Resources/Localizable.xcstrings`
and `GeckoTestBrowser/Dejavu/Support/L10n+Generated.swift` from the Android
app's `dejavu_strings.xml` files. `tools/dejavu_assets.py` makes the container and
Dejavu icons, the app icon, the colors and the launch screen from the Android
app's and Firefox's drawings (it needs Pillow and NumPy). Run them again after
those change.

### Sync sign-in

The Mozilla account sign-in uses the OAuth client of Firefox for Android with its
`https://accounts.firefox.com/oauth/success/...` address, which the sign-in tab
watches for. If Mozilla's servers refuse that address, Dejavu needs an OAuth
client of its own from Mozilla.
